  },
  {
    "file": "src/sprite/store.rs",
    "line": 164,
    "summary": "After a successful rename (or hard_link), Staged::drop still calls remove_file on the temp name, which no longer belongs to this call and may now be another writer's temp file.",
    "failure_scenario": "Two containers share a volume and both run the app as PID 1, so both have NEXT_TEMP=0 and the same temp name `x.tmp-1-0`. P1 renames its temp away, P2 creates a new `x.tmp-1-0`, then P1's drop deletes it. P2's rename or hard_link fails with ENOENT and its save is spuriously rejected. Every successful write_atomic also makes a wasted unlink syscall."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/sprite/store.rs",
    "line": 157,
    "summary": "The write_atomic doc says 'replace the destination' without saying that a symlink there is replaced rather than followed, or that the new file gets default permissions instead of the old file's.",
    "failure_scenario": "A user keeps `foo.sprites.json` as a symlink into a shared folder, or chmods it to 0600. Save (mod.rs:473 -> store::save) quietly turns it into a regular 0644 file next to the PNG, and the shared target never changes. extract.rs rejects symlinked map names for exactly this reason, but this doc gives store::save callers no warning, while the publish_new doc does cover symlinks."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/sprite/store.rs",
    "line": 160,
    "summary": "The durability sentence covers only the published name and leaves out that a crash can also leave `<name>.tmp-<pid>-<n>` files behind, which nothing ever cleans up.",
    "failure_scenario": "Power is lost between hard_link and Staged::drop's unlink (publish_new), or between staging and rename. After reboot a stray `.tmp-` file sits next to the sidecar or PNG for good, because create_new just skips that name. The existing tests and the epic promise 'no `.tmp-` left', and the doc doesn't mention this exception."
  },
  {
    "file": "docs/sprites.md",
    "line": 77,
    "summary": "The crash-durability limit now exists only in a code comment. rn-8v66.dq2 says it won't write crash durability into the manual because rn-y594 owns it, and rn-y594 says it won't touch docs/sprites.md, so the user manual never gets it.",
